an include adding salt, nitrates, nitrite or sugar, can involve smoking and flavoring the fish, and may include cooking it. The earliest form of curing fish was dehydration. Other methods, such as smoking fish or salt-curing also go back for thousands of years. The term \"cure\" is derived from the Latin curare, meaning to take care of. It was first recorded in reference to fish in 1743."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-cured-salmon"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cured_salmon"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["American Jewish cuisine","cuisine/american-jewish-cuisine",[["Lox","dish/lox"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Salmon","ingredient/salmon"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/9c/North_carolina_algonkin-essen02.jpg/500px-North_carolina_algonkin-essen02.jpg","w":500,"h":337,"by":"John White","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:North_carolina_algonkin-essen02.jpg"}},"ingredient/pine-nut":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/pine-nut","n":"Pine nut","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 5 dishes across 7 cuisines","intro":[["Pine nuts, also called piñón, pinoli (Italian: [piˈnɔːli]), or pignoli, are the edible seeds of pines (family Pinaceae, genus Pinus). According to the Food and Agriculture Organization, only 29 species provide edible nuts, while 20 are traded locally or internationally owing to their seed size being large enough to be worth harvesting; in other pines, the seeds are also edible but are too small to be of notable value as human food. The biggest exporters of pine nuts are China, Russia, North Korea, and Pakistan."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-pine-nut"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pine_nut"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":7,"groups":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine",[["Pi